---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadProfile } from '../lib/data';

const profile = loadProfile();
---
<Base
  title={`Colophon — ${profile.name}`}
  description={`How this site is built, and how its claims are checked.`}
>
  <p class="back"><a href="/">← {profile.name}</a></p>

  <h1>Colophon</h1>

  <h2>Sources and verification</h2>

  <p>
    Every figure quoted on this site is taken from the paper it describes and
    checked against that paper's own tables and text. Where a claim could not be
    supported by the source, it was removed rather than softened: two results
    that appear on earlier versions of my CV are absent here for that reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writing on this site is my own. I use AI tools for editing and for code
    assistance, as disclosed in the papers themselves where a venue requires it.
    References and reported numbers are verified against primary sources by hand
    before anything is published.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>

  <p>
    Static HTML generated with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a>, served
    from GitHub Pages. No client-side JavaScript, no analytics, no cookies, and
    no third-party requests of any kind — the fonts are self-hosted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Set in <a href="https://github.com/adobe-fonts/source-serif">Source Serif 4</a>,
    subset to the glyphs this site actually uses. Figures are converted from the
    papers' own LaTeX sources.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paper metadata lives in typed content files validated at build time. Two of
    those checks exist to prevent specific mistakes: one refuses to build if a
    preprint names a venue, and one requires any percentage or decimal shown on
    the site to record which section of which paper it came from.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .back { font-size: var(--size-sm); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  h2 { margin-top: var(--space-4); margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }
</style>
